WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.630
SIERRA: The Amazon is the biggest rainforest
in the world.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:12.210
If we were standing in the middle of it right
now, we’d be surrounded by 2.5 million species

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.670
of insects.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:19.380
The air would be thick with moisture, thanks
to 390 billion trees that exude gallons of

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.180
water every day.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.600
All around us would be thousands upon thousands
of species of plants and animals, many hidden,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.900
many yet undiscovered.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.969
There is also something present that we can’t
see or feel.

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:38.010
An enormous repository of carbon that’s
been sucked out of the atmosphere and safely

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.930
stored away.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.930
This place, The Amazon, is burning.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:50.020
From the summer of 2018 to the summer of 2019,
more than 3,700 square miles of the Amazon

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.690
were destroyed - a thirty percent increase
from the previous year... and things have

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.590
only gotten worse since the Covid pandemic
began.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:02.199
Without big changes soon, the Amazon as we
know it could be lost.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:05.420
And the carbon could be released.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:11.530
SKY NEWS: The Amazon Rainforest is on fire
again.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.470
Whole mountains, hills, and valleys engulfed
in smoke.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.759
PBS NEWSHOUR: Burning at a record-breaking
pace, sparking serious concerns around the

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:20.759
world.

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:22.620
TODAY: Scientists say we may have reached
a tipping point.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.090
If just three percent more of the Amazon is
destroyed...

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.470
there will be no turning back.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.119
SIERRA: I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is
Why It Matters.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:38.780
Today, the Amazon rainforest at a tipping
point, and the climate disaster that hangs

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.310
in the balance.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:48.490
We read about fires in the Amazon, frequently.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:49.899
So how are they started?

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:54.280
Are they sort of like the California wildfires
or is it something completely different?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:59.401
LOVEJOY: So actually, they're quite different
from wildfires in other parts of the world

00:01:59.401 --> 00:02:01.740
because the rainforest is wet.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.640
SIERRA: This is Dr. Tom Lovejoy, a Senior
Fellow at the United Nations Foundation and

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.509
a professor at George Mason University in
Virginia.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:17.260
He has worked in the Amazon since 1965, focusing
on scientific research, conservation, and

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.260
policy.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:24.319
LOVEJOY: Lightning strikes don't start fires
in the Amazon, people start fires.

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:30.090
And to give you a sense, once somebody has
cut down some forest, they have to wait for

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:36.300
five days without a drop of rain before it
is dry enough to set fire to and get rid of

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:43.019
all this stuff that's between the firebrand
and turning it into some kind of agricultural

00:02:43.019 --> 00:02:44.019
project.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:50.459
SIERRA: Nine countries share the Amazon, but
roughly sixty percent of it is within Brazil’s

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:51.459
borders.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:54.060
Which means they have a lot of control over
its fate.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.349
And today, across this vast territory, small
groups of people are intentionally setting

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:04.400
fires in order to dry out the land for logging,
and to clear it for agriculture.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:12.450
DE BOLLE: This way of clearing land for whatever
activity you want to do is very damaging to

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.709
the soil.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:19.840
So what happens is that if later you want
to reforest that particular patch of land,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.930
it becomes very hard to do because the soil
changes as soon as you clear it out because

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.810
it's now sustaining a different kind of environment
which is not rainforest-like.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.599
And then you can't really grow a rainforest
back in that sort of setting.

00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:38.890
SIERRA: This is Monica De Bolle, a senior
fellow at the Peterson Institute for International

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:44.220
Economics and a professor at the School of
Advanced International Studies at Johns Hopkins

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.220
University.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.310
DE BOLLE: People refer to the Amazon a lot
as the lungs of the world, but that's not

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.420
really what the Amazon is.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:57.629
What the Amazon does, is that it keeps a lot
of greenhouse gases from getting into the

00:03:57.629 --> 00:03:59.150
atmosphere.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:05.200
So it's not that the forest breathes for the
world, it's that once the forest is standing,

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.230
all of those greenhouse gases which are in
the soil, they're kept there, they're not

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.459
released into the atmosphere.

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:17.840
And as soon as you start cutting down the
forest, those greenhouse gases start to go

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.590
into the atmosphere - start to be released.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:22.970
It's sort of like a climate bomb.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:28.510
And as soon as you start cutting down the
rainforest, that bomb is released.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:33.230
SIERRA: So how big is that carbon bomb?

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.310
Really big.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:41.120
Scientists estimate that the Amazon stores
60 to 80 billion tons of carbon - or roughly

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.610
twice the total amount released from fossil
fuels in 2018 worldwide.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.870
Losing the Amazon would accelerate warming,
with harmful impacts felt around the world.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.699
DE BOLLE: The other issue is these hydrological
patterns.

00:04:56.699 --> 00:05:02.001
So when we say that we're referring to the
rain cycles, and it's not just local, because

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:09.470
given the size of the Amazon, again, it creates
not just a microclimate, but a sort of macroclimate

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:15.500
within the whole of the South American region,
even affecting a bit of Central America as

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.520
well, given that the Amazon is so far north.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.730
And so what happens is that the rain cycle
patterns as you cut down the forests, they

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:30.300
change, and sometimes they change really dramatically,
to the point where, you destroy people's lives

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.100
because they can no longer grow the crops
that they used to grow because the rain cycles

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.290
are completely different.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:42.940
That has already happened in many parts of
South America that have felt this direct impact.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.490
And this is only going to get worse, if the
deforestation continues.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:52.610
SIERRA: Hydrology is the study of the movement
and distribution of water.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:57.990
Because the Amazon is so large, and so wet,
its hydrology has a huge effect.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.520
It supplies water to almost every country
in South America.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.210
And, in fact, according to NASA, deforestation
has already been linked to reduced rainfall

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:08.380
in the region.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:15.590
DE BOLLE: And then on top of all that, you
have the issue of Amazon tipping point.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:22.190
So you get to a point where, if you go beyond
that point in terms of deforestation, the

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.630
rain forest is no longer self sustaining.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:30.590
It's going to turn into savanna simply because
of the ecological dynamics of how rainforests

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.980
behave.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:38.030
And there's a lot of concern that, where the
Amazon is right now is dangerously close to

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.550
this tipping point.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:44.870
SIERRA: And as ominous as the climate bomb
and chaotic rain cycles sound, these wouldn’t

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.979
be the only global consequences of an Amazon
tipping point.

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:53.180
SIERRA: I'd like to sort of have a bit of
a broader picture.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.790
What's at stake for the world when it comes
to the survival of the Amazon?

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:03.890
LOVEJOY: So what's at stake is that a significant,
probably the greatest concentration of terrestrial

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:10.150
biodiversity is in the Amazon, which by the
way is bigger than most people realize.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.780
It's essentially equal to the 48 contiguous
United States.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:20.560
So it's incredibly important in terms of biological
diversity and all that means for the future

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.599
of humanity and the future of nature.

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:28.660
SIERRA: He wouldn’t tell you this himself,
but Dr. Lovejoy is informally known as the

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.020
“godfather of biodiversity”.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.570
In fact he coined the term in 1980.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:39.520
It refers to the entire variety of life on
earth, and all the systems that make life

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:40.520
possible.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:47.110
The Amazon, home to 10 percent of all species,
is kind of like the capital city of biodiversity.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:52.900
SIERRA: Are there specific examples you can
point to that illustrate what humanity has

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.950
gained from the Amazon’s biodiversity?

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:59.870
LOVEJOY: Well, I would think that one that
would appeal almost to everybody in the world

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:00.949
is chocolate.

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:06.800
You know, I mean, cacao is native to the Amazon
and it was traded by indigenous peoples all

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.630
the way up into Mexico.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:15.039
Another would be the ACE inhibitors, which
hundreds of millions of people in the world,

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:20.350
probably at least a billion people take every
day to control their high blood pressure.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:26.940
And that all comes from understanding how
the venom of a nasty Viper actually works.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:33.219
And at any point, something may be discovered
about the biology of a particular species

00:08:33.219 --> 00:08:36.330
that turns out to have enormous practical
value.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:45.279
One example would be what the indigenous people
use to stun fish, which is actually a vine,

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:51.899
and the molecule in it that does that is curare,
big, long complicated molecule too expensive

00:08:51.899 --> 00:08:53.930
to synthesize.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:09:00.920
So actually, every major abdominal surgery
in the world uses curare as a muscle relaxant.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:07.480
SIERRA: The venom of a viper from a corner
of the rainforest led to a drug that is now

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:13.470
coursing through the veins of hundreds of
millions of people across the globe, helping

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:18.290
them combat heart disease, diabetes, kidney
disease, and even migraines.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:24.040
And it’s impossible to know how many other
breakthroughs lie undiscovered in the forest.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:28.840
LOVEJOY: So it's a library that most of the
books have not even been cataloged, let alone

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:29.840
read.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:36.720
And so you need to think about all that biodiversity
as a whole bunch of species, every day, inventing

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.380
new solutions to biological challenges in
its natural history.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:46.089
Any one of which if you pluck it off the shelf
and read it and understand it might have enormous

00:09:46.089 --> 00:09:47.899
practical benefit.

00:09:47.899 --> 00:09:53.490
So, to destroy the fundamental library for
the life sciences, is about as short sighted

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:57.209
a thing as humanity could possibly do.

00:09:57.209 --> 00:10:03.450
SIERRA: To understand why the fires and destruction
have accelerated, it’s a good idea to take

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:06.670
a look back at the history of the Brazilian
Amazon.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:13.600
DE BOLLE: It has always been pretty much a
no man's land in terms of regulation, in terms

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.019
of land rights.

00:10:15.019 --> 00:10:22.230
And thus, it has been subjected over the past
several decades, to exploitation in various

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:23.230
ways.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:29.529
In the 70s in the 80s, the Amazon was sort
of like the last frontier for development

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:30.529
in Brazil.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:37.779
And so in large part, roads, big highways,
were built across the Amazon region and there

00:10:37.779 --> 00:10:43.070
was a lot of deforestation that was done for
that, and after that development push, which

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:49.230
lasted a while, went away, then the cattle
growers came in and started deforesting some

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:54.649
areas and that was happening throughout the
1990s, and through the better part of the

00:10:54.649 --> 00:10:55.730
2000s.

00:10:55.730 --> 00:11:02.370
In 2004, that was the peak year of deforestation
in a very long time.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:08.540
And at that point, the Workers Party which
was governing Brazil, and the environmental

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:15.380
minister that was in place then, Marina Silva,
decided to put in place a plan to stop or

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.399
to halt deforestation.

00:11:17.399 --> 00:11:24.180
And so that government put together a strategy
to really reduce dramatically deforestation

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:28.839
and it was successful and they really enforced
environmental legislation.

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:37.360
They also put together a very good monitoring
system so deforestation fell by 80% in 10

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.180
years, as a result of these actions.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.090
SIERRA: I mean, that's a lot.

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:43.570
So what happened after that?

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:49.119
DE BOLLE: Brazil then dove into a deep recession
in 2015 and 2016.

00:11:49.119 --> 00:11:54.500
So these economic troubles basically meant
that all of these environmental concerns,

00:11:54.500 --> 00:12:01.050
issues, policies that had been put in place,
they became sort of not as important given

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:06.420
the other challenges that the country was
facing, and therefore, from about 2015 or

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:12.250
so, we did start to see deforestation pick
up again, and that was because the government

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:16.899
was trying to cut spending, so it couldn't
spend on a lot of these agencies that were

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:22.699
responsible for monitoring and for enforcing
environmental legislation.

00:12:22.699 --> 00:12:27.360
There were a lot of financing issues that
basically undid part of the effort that had

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:32.100
been done over those previous 10 years and
deforestation picked up again.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:38.470
SIERRA: So it was a mix of just not having
the money anymore to enforce these new rules

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:45.949
to, you know, oversee these new policies,
but also people looking to make money from

00:12:45.949 --> 00:12:46.949
the Amazon?

00:12:46.949 --> 00:12:47.949
DE BOLLE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:12:47.949 --> 00:12:54.120
I mean, that has always been a problem, because
what happens is that given a lot of distorted

00:12:54.120 --> 00:13:00.540
incentives, regulations, lack of land rights
and things like that, the land in the Amazon

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:06.529
is actually worth more if it has no trees
on it, than if it still has rainforests standing.

00:13:06.529 --> 00:13:12.220
SIERRA: And that’s because clearing rainforests
offers a quick dose of short-term profit.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:17.430
The lumber itself can be sold, and once cleared,
the land can be used to raise cattle and other

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:18.430
livestock.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:24.070
But economists like Monica note that this
is a backward way to value the land - when

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:29.070
compared to the potential profits of a discovery
like ACE inhibitors and the enormous cost

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:32.870
of climate change, these short term profits
are miniscule.

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:36.690
Still, they’re attractive to Brazilians
with few other options.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:41.610
SIERRA: Can you give me a sense of the scale
of the current destruction?

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:46.399
DE BOLLE: Every year you can see how much
land is deforested.

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:53.029
So in 2015, it started to pick up; it was
broadly the same in 2016, picked up a little

00:13:53.029 --> 00:13:56.010
bit more in 2017, 2018.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:14:00.720
And then we hit 2019, and in 2019, two things
happen.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.870
SIERRA: One, an exceptionally bad dry season,
which made the fires worse.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:10.140
And two, the election of Jair Bolsonaro as
president.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:14.410
Bolsonaro’s populist policies included an
emphasis on Brazil’s right to do whatever

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.170
it pleased with the Amazon.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:21.800
DE BOLLE: Throughout the presidential campaign
and then afterwards when he was elected, he

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:27.910
kept saying that the Amazon resources should
be exploited, kind of using a military dictatorship

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:33.269
sort of argument that, you know, this was
the last frontier of development in Brazil.

00:14:33.269 --> 00:14:39.020
So there were a lot of incentives from that
type of rhetoric for people to go in, do illegal

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:44.170
logging, start cutting down parts of the rainforest
to grow cattle and things of that sort.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:52.160
So the combination of a more severe dry season
and longer dry season with, you know, a presidential

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:59.839
posture that was unlike that of previous governments,
led to a big increase in deforestation in

00:14:59.839 --> 00:15:00.839
2019.

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:03.120
And we're seeing that again this year.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.880
SIERRA: So who benefits from clearing the
land in the Amazon?

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.400
DE BOLLE: These are small players, they're
the ones that are benefiting.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:15.019
Why is it not the big sort of agricultural
producers?

00:15:15.019 --> 00:15:19.380
Well, because the big agricultural producers
in Brazil actually know they have a lot to

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.570
lose if they start cutting down the rainforest.

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:27.339
In fact the big agricultural producers in
Brazil have made a lot of effort in getting

00:15:27.339 --> 00:15:33.420
sort of green seals, and you know, making
sure that whatever agriculture they practice

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.870
is environmentally friendly.

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:41.130
And this is what makes Brazil an agricultural
powerhouse and has made Brazil an agricultural

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:42.950
powerhouse over the years.

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:49.329
So we often see the Amazon issue being portrayed
in the media sometimes and blaming these big

00:15:49.329 --> 00:15:50.329
producers.

00:15:50.329 --> 00:15:52.430
The problem is not the big producers.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:58.310
And in fact, they have been at the forefront
of trying to convince the current government

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:04.000
that, you know, it's just bad policy to deforest
the Amazon because it hurts agricultural exports

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:05.139
in Brazil.

00:16:05.139 --> 00:16:10.829
SIERRA: This may sound surprising, but big
Brazilian companies have two strong motivations

00:16:10.829 --> 00:16:12.120
to end the fires.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.970
For one, global outrage is bad news for their
brands.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:20.910
But perhaps more importantly, the domino effect
of the fires on the ecosystem threatens to

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:25.500
change weather patterns, which could make
it hard to grow soybeans, or other commodities

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:26.500
for export.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:31.610
They’ve run the numbers, and the fires are
just bad for business.

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:36.339
Hearing all this, I sort of wonder about the
government's role in it.

00:16:36.339 --> 00:16:42.350
Do you think it's fair to say that deforestation
and the fires are a policy decision by the

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:43.699
Bolsonaro Government?

00:16:43.699 --> 00:16:49.120
LOVEJOY: So I would say, you know, de facto,
one has to conclude that, you know, if we

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:54.430
didn't have COVID right now, the fires in
the Amazon, which are worse than last year

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:56.569
would be the headlines.

00:16:56.569 --> 00:17:01.850
And there's just a lot of inadequate economic
analysis that goes into the things that they

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:03.389
allow to happen.

00:17:03.389 --> 00:17:08.680
But their, really, biggest fault is being
really weak on enforcement.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:14.220
You know, six or seven years ago, the Brazilian
government was actually quite strong in enforcing

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.419
their environmental laws.

00:17:16.419 --> 00:17:22.049
But budgets have been slashed, and wrong kinds
of signals have been sent.

00:17:22.049 --> 00:17:28.440
SIERRA: What motivation does Bolsonaro have
to sort of allow this all to happen or even

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:29.440
to encourage it?

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:33.289
What type of leader is he, like, what are
his political motivations for allowing this?

00:17:33.289 --> 00:17:39.330
DE BOLLE: So Bolsonaro is a president that
thrives on conspiracy theories, he thrives

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:40.870
on chaos.

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:44.480
He thrives on being anti-something.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:51.780
And so the anti-environment cause for him
has been, to some extent, important because

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:58.150
he has managed to create a narrative that
polarizes and divides the country and helps

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.130
him politically.

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:07.240
On the Amazon specifically, he has no understanding
and no interest in understanding the implications

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.950
of cutting down the rainforest.

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:15.680
To just underscore that, his current environmental
minister is somebody who has absolutely no

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:18.520
experience in this area.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:23.220
One thing that has happened in the Bolsonaro
administration, from the get go, this started

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:30.960
in the beginning, is that many of the agencies
that were involved in monitoring deforestation,

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:36.659
and enforcing environmental laws have been
stripped of their power to do so.

00:18:36.659 --> 00:18:43.360
But also, many people, including scientists,
leading scientists in the country, were fired

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:49.140
from their jobs for criticizing the government,
with respect to its environmental policies.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:54.330
SIERRA: Do you find that the argument for
this and the information that people need

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:59.260
to know about this gets trapped in this sort
of partisan space?

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:07.150
DE BOLLE: Brazil over the past several years
has become extremely polarized politically.

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:12.650
And so there are people who view environmental
issues as being something that we shouldn't

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:13.650
be talking about.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.170
It's not relevant.

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:17.890
And there are other people who think they're
highly relevant.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:23.950
And the way that this is split up in Brazilian
society is the people who don't view environmental

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:29.370
issues as being relevant are supporters of
the current government, and the remainder

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:30.370
are not.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:34.530
And so that doesn't help things at all, because
then you don't get a debate.

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:35.530
Right?

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:40.100
And then you can't really get the points across
and even the science across, in telling people,

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.690
look, but here are the facts.

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:42.690
These are the facts.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:47.830
If you cut down this rain forest to a significant
degree, it's gonna die out.

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:52.380
And if that happens, it's a disaster and it's
going to be a disaster for the country.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:57.250
It's very difficult to make those points on
the basis of the existing science simply because

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:59.990
the issue has become so politicized.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:05.330
SIERRA: Moving outside the political debate
in Brazil, there can be no doubt that the

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:10.700
loss of the Amazon would be an unmitigated
disaster for the rest of the world.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:16.260
And with a problem that has such dire consequences
for everyone on Earth, you’d think the international

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.110
community would have some say on the issue.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:24.299
But as a sovereign nation Brazil can do whatever
it likes with its Amazon.

00:20:24.299 --> 00:20:29.630
Sovereignty is a word that gets used often
in international relations and not very often

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:30.809
anywhere else.

00:20:30.809 --> 00:20:35.490
We’re going to take a quick break, but when
we return, a quick call with friend of the

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:41.510
pod Stewart Patrick, who just so happens to
be an expert on the concept of sovereignty,

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:46.760
and its friction with global problems like
climate change.

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:52.380
Hello listeners!

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:56.659
So if you dig all the questions we ask in
Why It Matters, you are going to love this

00:20:56.659 --> 00:21:00.750
other podcast brought to you by our friends
at Pushkin Industries.

00:21:00.750 --> 00:21:02.440
It’s called Solvable.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.679
Kinda a natural fit.

00:21:04.679 --> 00:21:10.309
Solvable is a weekly podcast where Malcolm
Gladwell, Anne Applebaum, and Jacob Weisberg

00:21:10.309 --> 00:21:14.610
ask how we can find solutions to some of the
biggest issues of our time.

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:19.740
You’ll hear from David Baltimore on AIDS,
Sal Khan on basic education, and Roseanne

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:21.900
Hagerty on homelessness.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.730
These problems can seem too big or too complicated
to fix.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:32.929
But in the hands of the right people -- activists,
scientists, policymakers and politicians at

00:21:32.929 --> 00:21:36.450
the top of their fields -- there are ways
to solve them.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:38.940
And that’s the hopeful message of Solvable.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.250
Listen and subscribe now in your favorite
podcasting app.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:44.549
SIERRA: Hi Stewart!

00:21:44.549 --> 00:21:47.429
PATRICK: Hey Gabby, how are you?

00:21:47.429 --> 00:21:48.730
SIERRA: Good, how are you?

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:49.730
PATRICK: Doing well!

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:50.730
What are we talking about today?

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.220
SIERRA: So I have a question.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.450
What is sovereignty?

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:59.169
PATRICK: Well, the concept of sovereignty
is basically the principle that every national

00:21:59.169 --> 00:22:03.900
government has supreme political authority
over its territory and its inhabitants, as

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:08.159
well as what crosses its borders, and no other
country has the right to tell it what it can

00:22:08.159 --> 00:22:09.159
do there.

00:22:09.159 --> 00:22:11.650
So it's akin to a no trespassing sign globally.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:16.550
But from an ecological perspective, the dilemma
arises that there's this big contradiction

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:21.340
between the international system, right, that's
the political world that's carved up into

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:24.400
193 different sovereign countries.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:30.169
And the Earth system, which is a natural world
that obeys no such boundaries, right?

00:22:30.169 --> 00:22:34.909
Now, the collision between these worlds is
most obvious in the case of global warming,

00:22:34.909 --> 00:22:39.820
because the emissions that are released anywhere
in any country affect everybody.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:42.380
They add to the planet's total.

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:47.309
But what if your territory contains something
that's so precious, that by exploiting it,

00:22:47.309 --> 00:22:49.250
you're threatening the future of others.

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:54.210
Something like the Amazon, right, which is
a priceless repository of biodiversity, as

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:59.590
well as a carbon sink that we humans are relying
on to absorb a lot of that co2 we've been

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:02.480
pumping into the atmosphere, then things get
a lot more complicated.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.990
And that's what's led to this controversy.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:08.789
Is Brazil, the owner of its portion of the
Amazon rainforest?

00:23:08.789 --> 00:23:10.920
Is it able to do what it wants to it?

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:16.340
Or is it only the Amazon's custodian with
an obligation to treat it with a level of

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.860
stewardship on behalf of all humanity?

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:20.650
So that's really the crux of this dispute.

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:24.870
Does the rest of the world have a say in what
Brazil and the other countries in the Amazon

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:27.340
basin actually do with their rainforest?

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:33.850
SIERRA: Okay so, the idea here is that if
something is on my land, I can do whatever

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:35.850
the heck I want with it.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:41.220
But what if I decide to burn leaves on my
lawn, and the smoke makes my neighbor’s

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:42.220
kids sick?

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:45.070
I’d probably be hearing from the police
pretty quickly.

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:50.340
Climate change is similar, on a much bigger
scale, and it presents a conundrum for traditional

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.380
ideas of sovereignty.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:57.049
Rising sea levels and soaring temperatures
do not obey borders.

00:23:57.049 --> 00:24:03.440
Still old norms are hard to change.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:08.320
What do you say to the claim that Brazil needs
to do this to better the lives of its own

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:12.279
people, potentially at the disservice of the
rest of the world?

00:24:12.279 --> 00:24:15.559
DE BOLLE: So that argument makes no sense
to me.

00:24:15.559 --> 00:24:17.779
Because there is no strategy.

00:24:17.779 --> 00:24:22.279
This government doesn't have a strategy towards
developing the Amazon that we could say, ‘Oh,

00:24:22.279 --> 00:24:23.279
this is good.’

00:24:23.279 --> 00:24:24.279
‘Oh, this is bad.’

00:24:24.279 --> 00:24:25.380
There isn't one.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:30.250
There's simply a lack of a strategy and a
view that, you know, this is an argument that

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:31.830
the President has often made.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:36.529
Oh, these are the poorest states in Brazil
and people deserve to use the Amazon as they

00:24:36.529 --> 00:24:37.529
please.

00:24:37.529 --> 00:24:40.820
So this is the sort of thing that we hear
but in the end, it makes absolutely no sense

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:44.110
because everybody loses including Brazil itself.

00:24:44.110 --> 00:24:49.289
If cutting down the rainforest is going to
change the climate, the hydrological cycles,

00:24:49.289 --> 00:24:54.179
all of these things that I talked about, and
given that Brazil is an agricultural powerhouse,

00:24:54.179 --> 00:24:59.330
it's a very important agricultural exporter,
changing the hydrological cycle is going to

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:00.750
affect other crops.

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:06.039
It's going to affect the big soybean producers,
it's going to affect other types of agricultural

00:25:06.039 --> 00:25:11.900
producers around the country, especially those
who are closest to the Amazon biome.

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:17.240
So it's not in the national interest at all
to make the sovereignty argument to allow

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:19.390
deforestation, it just doesn't make any sense.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:25.559
SIERRA: So you feel like, you know, the claim
that this is lifting up Brazil's poor doesn't

00:25:25.559 --> 00:25:26.640
hold any water...

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:27.920
DE BOLLE: Absolutely.

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:34.490
It's completely short term and short sighted,
it's not helping the poor at all.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:36.039
It's not giving them anything.

00:25:36.039 --> 00:25:41.730
And if you think about this in in a medium
term sense, if you are scrapping this natural

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:47.540
resource, which has a lot of benefits for
this population that's very poor, then what's

00:25:47.540 --> 00:25:51.610
going to happen to them over the medium term
is that, you know, their living conditions

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.730
are going to become even worse.

00:25:53.730 --> 00:26:00.309
SIERRA: So yes, Brazil can do whatever it
wants with the Amazon, based on its sovereign

00:26:00.309 --> 00:26:01.309
rights.

00:26:01.309 --> 00:26:05.360
But it’s not one of those classic sovereignty
problems in which what’s good for a single

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:08.919
country conflicts with what’s good for everyone
else.

00:26:08.919 --> 00:26:13.600
The destruction of the Amazon isn’t even
good for Brazil.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:18.280
What responsibility do you think that Brazil
has to the rest of the planet when it comes

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:21.240
to preventing climate change and preserving
biodiversity?

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.880
DE BOLLE: I think Brazil has a huge responsibility.

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:31.500
And what's sad, I think, is that previous
governments actually recognized that responsibility

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:36.120
and used it to increase Brazil's diplomatic
power.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:40.020
Brazil is not exactly a diplomatic powerhouse
in the world.

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:44.289
But it did have this soft power when it came
to environmental issues.

00:26:44.289 --> 00:26:49.580
It was very influential in putting together
the Paris Climate Agreement precisely because

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:56.399
it is a country that has all of these resources
and has this massive rain forest.

00:26:56.399 --> 00:27:01.410
So previous governments knew how to use this
and use this in the right way, you know, with

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:02.410
the right perception.

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:07.299
Yes, we have the responsibility, and with
the responsibility comes this soft power that

00:27:07.299 --> 00:27:09.230
we really do want to use.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:13.090
This is a perception that's completely lost
on the current government.

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:17.799
The current government does not see Brazil
as having any kind of responsibility when

00:27:17.799 --> 00:27:23.350
it comes to the rest of the world, nor does
it perceive this soft power as being important.

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:24.649
It just doesn't.

00:27:24.649 --> 00:27:31.440
And so this kind of attitude has been undermining
to a very large extent, Brazil’s standing

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:36.740
in different fora around the world when it
comes to climate change and climate issues.

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:42.240
SIERRA: There has been some collective action
by the countries that share the Amazon.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:48.650
In 2019, seven representatives met in Colombia
to sign the Leticia Pact, an agreement created

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:53.880
to address the issue of forest fires through
coordinated responses, including monitoring

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:56.350
and developing educational initiatives.

00:27:56.350 --> 00:28:01.110
The pact also sought to increase the role
of the Amazon’s indigenous communities in

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.090
sustainable development.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:06.700
But many other countries have remained silent.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:10.529
Do you think this is an issue that should
be a priority for other countries?

00:28:10.529 --> 00:28:14.120
LOVEJOY: You know, I think there's absolutely
no question about that.

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:15.919
The answer is yes.

00:28:15.919 --> 00:28:22.340
This is too big a piece of how the climate
system works, and too big a piece of the biological

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:28.400
diversity of the planet for it not to be in
the interest of countries which can afford

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:33.679
it to contribute to improving the future trajectory
of what's going on there.

00:28:33.679 --> 00:28:40.200
And that that's happened before in 1990, the
G7 meeting in Houston, basically launched

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:44.620
a program for the Brazilian rainforest, funded
through the World Bank.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:50.640
It did a lot of good things, demarcated a
lot of the indigenous areas in Brazil, and

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:54.020
there's no reason that this kind of thing
can't happen again.

00:28:54.020 --> 00:28:59.280
SIERRA: Is there a better way for the country
to benefit from having the Amazon there rather

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.360
than obviously just cutting it down?

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:02.970
DE BOLLE: Absolutely.

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:07.120
There's no lack of proposals to deal with
Amazon.

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:12.880
Over the last several decades, different environmental
ministers and other environmental activists,

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:19.460
NGOs, researchers, scientists, you know, there's
just so much material that's been published

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:25.190
on the Amazon, and how to deal with the problems
that the Amazon faces, and how do you actually

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:27.679
address, you know, these long standing issues.

00:29:27.679 --> 00:29:33.779
I think that the main starting point for trying
to get things right in the Amazon is the land

00:29:33.779 --> 00:29:36.330
registration and the land rights issue.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.539
Until you sort that out, you can't sort anything
else out.

00:29:39.539 --> 00:29:46.320
SIERRA: Clearing up land registration would
help end the Amazon’s no-man’s-land status,

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:50.250
and start the ball rolling toward a smarter
valuation of the land.

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:54.750
This means thinking about the long-term value
of the land’s biodiversity, rather than

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:58.440
it’s short-term value as cleared grazing
land.

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:06.000
LOVEJOY: So my dear friend and colleague Carlos
Nobre in Brazil has been talking about an

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:08.140
intact rain forest bioeconomy.

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:14.289
And yes, there are things you can do like
aquaculture, because some of the Amazon fish

00:30:14.289 --> 00:30:19.419
could ultimately become as well known as salmon
or cod around the world.

00:30:19.419 --> 00:30:25.669
But then there is a need for something which
Carlos calls the “MIT of the Amazon”,

00:30:25.669 --> 00:30:32.210
which would be a Research Institute, which
is dedicated to developing some of these secrets

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:41.169
locked away in the biology and a really interesting
example could be to study leaf cutting ants.

00:30:41.169 --> 00:30:47.620
These are ants that have colonies of maybe
a million, they will defoliate trees overnight.

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:51.279
And you see all the ants marching along with
little bits of green leaves.

00:30:51.279 --> 00:30:56.980
Well, what they're doing is taking those down
into their nest where they have a giant underground

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:58.280
fungus farm.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.970
And then they feed on the fungus, that's their
agriculture.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:07.030
Well, for sure the ants have been able to
figure out which trees have natural fungicides

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:13.010
in their leaves, which it makes sense to leave
alone and not take down to the fungus farm.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:19.750
So that could give you a direct way to find
a whole variety of new fungicides then can

00:31:19.750 --> 00:31:26.640
be helpful in agriculture and other medical
applications to add to the ones that we already

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:27.640
have.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:31.980
SIERRA: Fungicide may sound like small potatoes,
but it’s not.

00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:37.720
In 2019 the global market for fungicides was
$12.3 billion dollars.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.500
That's pretty cool.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:43.610
I would definitely apply to go to the MIT
of the Amazon.

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:46.960
LOVEJOY: I think it could be a really exciting
place.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:53.559
SIERRA: I mean, to put it a bit crudely, the
idea there is just stop cutting down the Amazon

00:31:53.559 --> 00:31:57.240
and start patenting the technology that it
produces.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:02.300
LOVEJOY: That's true and the other really
important thing to think about is how you

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:07.970
can have sustainable cities in the Amazon,
where people can have a reasonable quality

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:11.320
of life and do it without destroying the forest.

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:16.990
SIERRA: One of these cities already exists,
it is called Manaus.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:22.160
It’s...amazing and you should probably Google
some images of it when you get a chance.

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:26.860
A thriving city deep in the Amazon, interwoven
with the trees.

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:30.370
It even hosted matches of Brazil’s World
Cup in 2014.

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:36.760
LOVEJOY: Most of the industry in Manaus is
actually assembly, and they assemble things

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:40.529
using materials that are not from the forest.

00:32:40.529 --> 00:32:45.029
So the largest Harley Davidson factory in
the world is in Manaus.

00:32:45.029 --> 00:32:51.710
Most of the computer boards and similar devices
for cell phones are assembled in Manaus.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:58.289
So that's created economic opportunity and
the state of Amazonas, where Manaus is, has

00:32:58.289 --> 00:33:03.909
an incredibly low deforestation rate because
opportunity is in Manaus.

00:33:03.909 --> 00:33:13.139
I think most of the leaders of Amazon countries
want to see a sustainable future for the Amazon.

00:33:13.139 --> 00:33:16.720
That's what they signed to agree to in the
Laticia Pact.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:22.539
I think we know enough of the pieces of how
you could put that together, that a program

00:33:22.539 --> 00:33:28.271
led by the Inter-American Development Bank,
which the Laticia Pact has asked for, could

00:33:28.271 --> 00:33:31.340
actually make a serious difference.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:37.179
And there are enough countries in the developed
world, I think of Norway in particular, who

00:33:37.179 --> 00:33:42.029
are really interested in the future of the
tropical forests of the world and the Amazon,

00:33:42.029 --> 00:33:45.090
who would be willing to contribute.

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:51.620
SIERRA: Are there any forms of progress that
you're hopeful about, you know, overarching

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:54.600
moves for change in this whole storyline?

00:33:54.600 --> 00:34:00.429
DE BOLLE: What I'm most hopeful about is that
I do see that in the private sector in Brazil,

00:34:00.429 --> 00:34:05.419
climate change and environmental issues have
become priorities.

00:34:05.419 --> 00:34:12.429
By and large, the businesses in Brazil are
very worried about how the country is perceived

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:18.230
and how they, themselves, are perceived as
part of the country in sort of fighting for

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:22.659
these causes, climate change, environmental
problems, and all that.

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:28.200
So that I think brings an element of hope
because if there's another push from some

00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:32.980
kind of engagement with other countries, the
private sector in Brazil would be largely

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:34.620
supportive of that.

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:36.230
Even if the government's doing nothing.

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:42.220
So, for example, when it comes to greenhouse
emissions, Brazil doesn't have, like the US

00:34:42.220 --> 00:34:47.619
has, any sort of monitoring or anything like
that over greenhouse emissions but the companies

00:34:47.619 --> 00:34:48.750
do it themselves.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:53.919
They do it, and there's a private registry
of greenhouse emissions that's done by the

00:34:53.919 --> 00:34:54.919
companies.

00:34:54.919 --> 00:34:56.710
They report on their own on what they emit.

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:59.000
And so that's the part that makes me hopeful.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:05.540
I mean, in spite of the inclinations of this
government, by and large, there's a perception

00:35:05.540 --> 00:35:10.680
in Brazil's private sector, big companies,
small companies, medium sized companies, that

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:14.490
these are really important issues and that
you can't overlook them and that the future

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.390
of the country depends on them.

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:19.570
SIERRA: So there are reasons to be hopeful.

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:25.270
Many Brazilians understand the true value
of this land, to themselves and to the world.

00:35:25.270 --> 00:35:27.420
Brazil’s industries understand it too.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:33.380
And with the right international pressure,
the fires and deforestation could stop.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:38.930
The problem is that the world is really distracted,
more distracted than at any time in recent

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:39.930
history.

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:44.260
And it remains deeply unclear whether that
pressure will actually materialize.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:50.380
LOVEJOY: I think what it comes down to is
most of the people who are doing things don't

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:53.130
think that their increment is going to make
much of a difference.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:57.290
But when you add it all up, it's at a global
scale.

00:35:57.290 --> 00:36:00.050
So there's a real moment of reckoning going
on.

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:06.020
But now is the time to actually do something
about that and end up with a soft, sustainable

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:08.210
landing.

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:25.800
Global News: [protest chants] “ele não,
Amazônia sim!”

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:36.790
“not him [Bolsanaro], Amazon yes!”

